SHAPE: Two entries in "paragraphs", both plain prose with no bullet lines. First: a short plain passage that names all of the report's companies together in its opening sentence and says how crowded or concentrated the field is. Second: for each company that has a listed fact, one or two complete sentences naming that company and saying what it did through its own action and what that changes for buyers and for the rest of the market; a company with no listed fact appears only in the first entry's naming sentence and is never given a detail the facts do not supply. Every company appears inside a complete sentence that names it: no label fragment that splits a company's name away from its sentence, and no bullet lines for companies.

### 7. conclusion  |  heading: "Conclusion and Outlook for the Wealth Management Platform Market"  |  about 109 words
Purpose (what the reader learns here): The market's growth, its shift to hybrid and robo advice, its bank and US strongholds and its busy field of vendors all point the same way: platforms keep spreading as more people, including young starters, invest through them.
Facts to use:
- [fs.opportunities.1] Continued technological advancements in big data analytics and AI create lucrative business opportunities for market players. (CMI report)
Keywords:
  - REQUIRED secondary keyword: one sentence of this section must contain all three words "outlook", "wealth management platform" and "market" (reorder them as the sentence needs). A synonym does not count: an "opening" is not "opportunities", "how big" is not "size", and a sentence that says "the main drivers" without naming the market does not carry the keyword. Put that sentence in one paragraph only.
SHAPE: One paragraph that pulls the post together using only points already made. It contains no digits at all: it refers back to earlier numbers in words (the forecast above, steady growth, a modest pace). It still uses the market's full name once. Its last sentence is the CMI analyst's view.

RULES THAT APPLY EVERYWHERE
- Voice: plain everyday English for a general reader. Short sentences with one idea each, averaging about 15 words and none longer than 25. Prefer the everyday word (use, show, buy, grow, help) to the formal one. A bullet is one or two short sentences, never one long one. Contractions are fine. No slang, hype, calls to action or direct address ("you"). The first time a technical term is unavoidable, explain it in a few words; if a sentence works without it, leave it out. Every sentence is one complete grammatical sentence: its subject is a noun phrase with a single head noun set up by an article or another determiner, and its main verb follows within a few words of that noun; when a subject would name several things, the sentence says how they relate, with prepositions or a verb, or it is split into two sentences; when a sentence compares two things, it names both sides in full, and the two sides are of the same kind.
- Actors: say who does what. The subject of a sentence is the person, company, group or thing that acts (buyers, vendors, teams, regulators, a technology), and its verb is active. A source's name goes in a short clause beside the fact, never in a frame that makes a report or a website the subject of a sentence about the market. Use a passive verb only when nobody can be named as the actor. A sentence states a fact with its actor as the subject and an active verb; target at most one passive sentence in six.
- Clear reference: every sentence can be understood by itself. No sentence begins with a pointing word (this, that, these, those, such), whether or not a noun follows it, and none begins with a bare pronoun that refers back to an earlier sentence or idea. To connect a sentence to the one before it, name the thing again with its own noun as the subject, or join the two ideas with a connecting word inside one sentence. A reader who lands on any single sentence must know what it is about without reading the one before it.
- Facts: state only the facts listed for a section. A fact from the CMI report may be stated plainly. A fact whose source is not CMI (news evidence, or a figure marked NOT CMI) names that source, and the month when it has one, in the same sentence and is never credited to CMI. Every sentence that carries a number from such a fact names the source inside that very sentence, even when the sentence before it already did. Every sentence that gives one of another source's figures carries the source's name again in itself — "The same source", "That source" and "It" pointing back at a source are never written. A fact marked historical carries its year and is never presented as current. A figure appears once in the whole post: never repeat one from an earlier section, the conclusion included; refer back to it in words. A sentence that gives a number says what the number measures. Never write a figure, company detail or claim that no listed fact supports; where a section has few facts, it stays short. The "Purpose" line of a section tells you what the reader should learn; it is not a source of facts.
- Explaining: you may add plain sentences that explain what a fact means, what a term means or why it matters to the market, as long as they add no figure, name, date or claim of their own. This is how a section with few facts still reads as a full, useful section.
- Never talk about the writing process: no mention of "the facts", "the plan", "the report not saying", "what is not available" or what you could not find. Leave out what you cannot support, quietly.
- Placement: put each fact in the sentence that makes the section's point. At most one fact per sentence, and no chains of "according to".
- No filler: every clause carries information. A clause whose only job is to tell the reader what they will see, understand or learn, or to announce why a fact is being given, is left out. A sentence ends when its fact ends. A list lead-in states the split plainly (who splits what, into how many parts) and nothing about its purpose.
- Flow: each section's first sentence picks up from the section before it. After the transition the post stays on the market. The guest article's context belongs to the opening alone: a market section uses a guest point only when that section lists one as a guest example, and then in one sentence.
- Fresh writing: put every fact in your own words and sentence shapes; never reuse a run of ten or more words from a fact or the report as written — this includes lists of segment categories, which you break up by writing the connective words your own way and keeping a few of your own words between the split and its categories. A fact that arrives as a full sentenced statement (a driver, a restraint, a U.S. statement, a company note) is research wording, not a line of prose: say its meaning again in your own words, keeping only its figures; its sentence shape must not survive into the draft. Similarly, when a fact lists many companies, name only the few that matter in that sentence (at most six), grouped or ordered your own way, never the whole list in the order given.
- Primary keyword: use the full market name once in every market section, the conclusion included, and never twice in the same paragraph. The sentence that carries a section's secondary keyword may be that one use. Elsewhere in the section say "the market", "this market" or "it". It is plain text everywhere except the link in the opening. The market is an industry category made of many companies' products: never say or imply that it is a product, software or a company.
- Secondary keyword: where a section lists one, every word of it must appear together in one sentence of that section — that section is not finished without it, whatever its facts allow. Each keyword belongs to its section alone: its words sit in one paragraph there, never spread over two, and when the same idea comes up in other sections it is said in different words ("openings", "the biggest opening", the word the world uses), never in a listed keyword's words. You may reorder a keyword's words to fit the sentence; you may not replace one with a synonym. Use no keyword outside the section it is listed for, so a paragraph never ends up playing host to two sections' keywords.
- Long-tail search: where a section lists one, that section is not finished until one of its sentences contains every content word of it, whatever its facts allow. The words may be REORDERED to read well, with only light words (a, the, of, in, for, and) between them, and acronyms and proper nouns are written in their normal capitalisation (AI, US) even though the phrase is listed in lowercase; a synonym never counts. The sentence is about something real in the section: the phrase is never the subject of a sentence about itself, never a label, never a row of the sentence's own topic words, and never placed after a colon or comma. The phrase goes only into an ordinary slot of that sentence: an object of a verb, an object of a preposition, or the subject whose single head noun is one of the phrase's own nouns with its other words as modifiers, never the phrase pasted in its listed order as a pile of nouns — and the same placement holds for any assigned secondary keyword.
- Related terms are optional: use one where it fits a sentence you would write anyway.
- CMI analyst opinion: one or two sentences in the whole post, no more. The conclusion's last sentence is always one. Attribute it by role ("a CMI analyst", "CMI's research team"), never by name or in quotation marks. It is a judgement a reader could disagree with, built only from facts already in the post, and it adds no new fact. Its comparison is complete: both sides of a contrast inside it are actions or properties of the same kind, or the contrast is left out.
- Recency: call something recent, new or current only if its source places it within the 12 months before today's date.
- Length: the whole post comes to 1350 words, give or take 10 percent. Count roughly as you write: if the post is running to the outer ends of the section budgets, add a plain explanatory sentence where one already belongs, never filler; never hand in sections of half the budget.

OUTPUT CHECKLIST
All seven market sections are present in the given order with their given headings. The opening has the link once and the placeholder once. Every figure is in a listed fact. No section states a fact that is not listed. Every assigned keyword is in its section, and every section's listed long-tail phrase is used in that section.

